\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET SNAPSHOT: U.S. Stocks Slump As Japan Clamps Down On Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Donna Kardos Yesalavich  \par \uc2 612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 16:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (MarketWatch) -- U.S. stocks struggled for gains Wednesday, hampered by gains in the dollar following Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to try to stem the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average (DJI) reversed higher, adding 11 points to 10,538.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite (RIXF) lost 0.1%. The Standard & Poor's 500 index (SPX) fell 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 The declines came as a wave of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  totaling nearly $12 billion sent the dollar sharply higher against the yen, a successful beginning to what appears to be a campaign by Tokyo to stop a soaring yen that threatens to cripple its economy. The move marked the first time Japanese authorities publicly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since 2004.\par \par \uc2 Market players were surprised by the scope of the \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as signs that the famously conservative Bank of Japan would allow the extra yen to stay in the economy--a bold move by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  standards that could pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. Analysts saw this new accommodation as an important measure to increase Japan's monetary base as a further weapon to fight a long deflationary spiral that has dragged down the economy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose more than 3% to trade above 85 yen following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The U.S. Dollar Index (DXY) , tracking the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a basket of six others, climbed 0.6%.\par \par \uc2 "As the dollar increases, that cuts the appetite for risk," which weighed on riskier areas of the market including stocks and commodities, said Peter Cardillo, chief market economist at Avalon Partners. Crude-oil futures fell below $76 a barrel and gold futures also dropped.\par \par \uc2 Treasurys, meanwhile, were mixed. The two-year note edged up, pushing its yield down to 0.48%, while the 10-year note slipped, lifting its yield (UST10Y) to 2.69%.\par \par \uc2 In overseas markets, Japan's Nikkei Stock Average of 225 companies surged after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while most major European indexes were lower. Pressuring European markets, claimant-count unemployment in the U.K. rose in August for the first time since January this year, hinting at tough times ahead once government spending cuts begin to bite in earnest.\par \par \uc2 Among U.S. data, a report on New York-area manufacturing disappointed. The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2 's Empire State Manufacturing Survey showed New York manufacturing activity is expanding this month but at a slower pace than in August, while expectations about economic activity in the New York region continued to deteriorate. The report's expectations index fell to its lowest reading since March 2009.\par \par \uc2 Separate data showed U.S. industrial production rose a second straight month in August, but the increase was smaller than expected. Capacity utilization also increased, although operating rates remain well below the 1972-2009 average. Meanwhile, U.S. import prices rose more than expected in August from July amid higher energy and food prices, a move that should help quell fears that the economy may fall into deflation.\par \par \uc2 Among stocks in focus, \uc2 Steel Dynamics\uc2  (STLD) fell 0.6% after the steelmaker issued a third-quarter earnings target that badly missed Wall Street's expectations. The company said results were hurt by lower margins in the steel segment, although it hinted a turnaround was possible.\par \par \uc2 Pall Corp\uc2 . (PLL) jumped 7%. The maker of filtration and water-purification products posted a 21% drop in fiscal fourth-quarter profit as one-time charges doubled, although revenue and margins rose. The company forecast fiscal-year earnings above analysts' estimate.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2010 10:16 ET (14:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100915e69f003nc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
