\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Yen Falls Hard After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,355 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 20:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 2:47 (Dow Jones) The yen plummeted against the dollar Wednesday after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for the first time in more than six years. The greenback gained more than 3% after on Tuesday it hit a 15-year low against the yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again Thursday if necessary. The euro ticked down only slightly against the dollar, likely because speculation still swirls as to whether the Fed will initiate another round of asset purchases -- known as quantitative easing -- to kickstart a moribund US economy, says Vassili Serebriakov of \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . Talk of further Fed action has weighed on the dollar. (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:36 (Dow Jones) Shares of oil and gas services companies surge on US government's announcement that wells sitting idle in Gulf of Mexico for five or more years must be plugged. About 3,500 wells and 650 platforms are immediately affected by the mandate, which means new business for companies involved in well decommissioning. Marine contractor Cal Dive International (DVR) jumps 19% to $5.87, while \uc2 Tetra Technologies\uc2  (TTI) and Global Industries (GLBL) gain 18% and 16%, respectively. \uc2 Superior Energy Services\uc2  (SPN) also up 7.6%. The new rule becomes effective Oct. 15. (adam.cancryn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:25 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Time Warner Cable\uc2  (TWC) shares drop 4.9% to $52.46 after downbeat remarks from CFO \uc2 Robert Marcus\uc2  at a West Coast conference. Marcus says "subscriber environment very, very weak," thanks to high unemployment, high owned and rental home vacancies and "really anemic new home formation." Says that has so far resulted in "some pretty weak subscriber numbers" in 3Q. TWC second-largest decliner in S&P 500. (maxwell.murphy@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2:08 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) on Wednesday announces new features for Internet Explorer 9, the newest version of its Internet browser. Available today in a pre-release version, IE9 supports the hardware-acceleration features in HTML5 for faster online response. The idea is to create a web experience that feels more like working in a PC application. A Bing product manager shows off Bing viewed through IE9, revealing that search engine's trademark splashy homepage photo is now a splashy homepage video. IE currently has about 60% of the global browser market but has been losing market share for years. MSFT up 0.4% to $25.14. (jeanette.borzo@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:57 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Cisco\uc2  (CSCO) announcing it will begin paying a dividend garnered much positive attention, which surprised Chad Brand, founder and president of Peridot Capital. \uc2 Cisco\uc2  says dividend yield will be 1% to 2%, which is "unimpressive and unimportant," Brand says on his blog. "For the investment strategists who claim that income-oriented investors will now all of the sudden flock to \uc2 Cisco\uc2  shares, they are clearly overstating the situation," he writes. "Until tech companies start paying dividends that rival those in sectors notorious for fat dividends, such as consumer staples and utilities (3-5% per year), there will be little reason for income-seeking investors to all of the sudden embrace technology stocks." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.peridotcapitalist.com/2010/09/cisco-dividend-initiation-very-overhyped.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.peridotcapitalist.com/2010/09/cisco-dividend-initiation-very-overhyped.html}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1:37 (Dow Jones) \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says \uc2 Time Warner Cable\uc2  (TWC) and Cox Communications may swap assets in order to cluster their West Coast cable systems, but a broader alliance between the cable giants, or M&A, is unlikely. The companies have reportedly held talks about an alliance, which could help them in programming negotiations with network owners. The maturing cable TV industry has shown a penchant for consolidation, but \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says the most likely outcome of the talks is a swap for TWC's systems in San Diego in return for Cox's systems in Los Angeles, which could allow both companies to cut back on operating costs. (nat.worden@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:30 (Dow Jones) US stocks maintain moderate gains in early afternoon trading, with relatively defensive consumer staples and health-care sectors leading the advance. Tech sector's also solid, while energy, materials and utilities all decline. Gains for Dow Industrials paced by Travelers, IBM, \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  and Kraft. UTX, \uc2 Chevron\uc2  and Exxon among the notable stocks in retreat. Stocks tracked the euro higher early on as US dollar eased off morning highs, and they continue to mirror moves in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . DJIA up 30; Nasdaq gains 8; S&P 500 up 2. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:11 (Dow Jones) Retailer Canadian Tire's (CTC.A.T) refinements to its management-team structure, announced earlier to day, and shift to an integrated company with six business categories, should help improve the speed of decision making longer term, BMO Capital says. But it could also cause nearer-term disruption as certain roles are eliminated and others expanded, firm says. "That said, we are left wondering why these decisions were not made earlier in the year," BMO says. Recently in Toronto, Canadian Tire down 0.8% at C$56.45. (andy.georgiades@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1:05 (Dow Jones) What lies ahead for Beazer (BZH)? Analysts seem divided on that question after BZH said quarterly orders will likely fall short of previous expectations. "We expect sharply lower closings in the coming quarters based on the weak orders and backlog," \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. \uc2 UBS\uc2  sticks with positive outlook, saying risk/reward is "compelling" and current valuation reflects an attractive entry point "for investors looking to participate in a housing rebound," firm writes. BZH down 6.4% at $4.25. News rattling entire sector: \uc2 Standard Pacific\uc2  (SPF) falls 3%; \uc2 KB Home\uc2  (KBH) down 2.3%. (dawn.wotapka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:57 (Dow Jones) McKesson (MCK) shares gain 6% to $63.30 after pharmaceutical supplier says it'll hit FY guidance despite slowing health-care volumes. Comments came during MCK's two-day, three-conference tour, where Bernstein says it reassured investors about its revenue growth. "McKesson has been very vocal and very active about the achievability of their estimates," firm adds. Aiding company's claims is report released today showing sequential gains in August prescription volumes compared with July, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says. \uc2 AmerisourceBergen\uc2  (ABC), which faces similar volume concerns, also up 4.4% to $30.37 on the positive commentary. (adam.cancryn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:49 (Dow Jones) Medical-device and generic drug-maker \uc2 Covidien\uc2  (COV) has been busily remaking its portfolio with purchases and divestitures since splitting from \uc2 Tyco International\uc2  (TYC) about three years ago. Amid several smaller deals, COV bought vascular company ev3 this summer for $2.6B. Looking ahead, COV is targeting more "tuck-in" deals, and "likely not as much activity as you've seen in the past," CEO \uc2 Richard Meelia\uc2  says during investor conference. And no more business sales for a while. "We see no divestitures we would execute on in the coming fiscal year," Meelia says. Big issue at today's conference is guidance for FY11, but that comes after closing bell. COV up 1.4% to $38.20. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 12:45 (Dow Jones) Tax advisers like the potential impact on their own personal income taxes from the Tax Hike Prevention Act, introduced by Senate Republican leader Mitch McConnell to prevent rate increases in 2011. But, they doubt the success of efforts like McConnell's to keep the Bush tax cuts in place. No matter which party controls Congress after the elections, "we are going to end up with some tax increases for 2011 given the state of the budget and that Obama will still be President," says \uc2 David Shechtman\uc2 , a partner at \uc2 Drinker Biddle & Reath LLP\uc2 . That's the party line for advisers working to help wealthy clients get ready for the hikes. (arden.dale@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Equities Market Talk Editors, \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Paul Vigna, 212 416-2184,paul.vigna@dowjones.com \par Visit the Market Talk website at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.djnmarkettalk.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.djnmarkettalk.com}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-15-10 1447ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100915e69f000it\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
