\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japanese  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Will Likely Have A Sequel\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 837 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 20:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "shock and awe" overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets has been effective, but it's almost certain to be repeated to ensure efforts to slow the yen's appreciation are successful.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities said they were prepared to extend the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the U.S. session as well as Thursday in Tokyo if deemed necessary. So far, there has been little evidence to suggest Japan had been active in North American \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Wednesday, but more yen selling is considered likely by analysts.\par \par \uc2 That likelihood is reinforced by the fact the conditions that created yen strength against the dollar, notably the flows into the yen because of its safe-haven status and the flattening of global interest rates, aren't likely to end soon.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's isolation in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which it's undertaking without the co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  of any other central banks, also argues success beyond Japan's initial volley could be elusive.\par \par \uc2 It's by no means certain \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be drawn into the kind of sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign that marked their last foray into markets in 2003-04, during which the government spent Y35 trillion over 15 months to slow the yen's appreciation. But to meet the minimal standards of credibility, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely have to sell yen again.\par \par \uc2 "In the initial round you want to have credibility, and credibility is mostly defined by success," said Robert Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 The extent of near-term \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  required is partly contingent on the price action, he said. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drives the dollar higher against yen, there's less need for added action. Should the dollar quickly retrace its gains versus the yen, there will be a need for more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.\par \par \uc2 If history is a guide, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be heavy in the next few sessions.\par \par \uc2 When \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  began \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in January 2003, they did so for three days in a row and a total of eight times in the month, according to a report from Jessica Hoverson, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 The need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be curtailed if fundamental forces in the market shifted to induce a weakening in the yen against the dollar. That's not something many analysts consider likely, at least not in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Relative growth and interest rates have been signaling for some time the yen should weaken, but that hasn't happened.\par \par \uc2 "Instead, you have this environment where the convergence of global interest rates towards those in Japan is curbing capital outflows from Japan and allowing those flows that do leave to be hedged at a reduced cost," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Lynch.\par \par \uc2 Low interest rates globally and narrow rate differentials between Japan and other countries also support the yen by removing the incentive for using the yen in carry trades, said a report from Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and public policy strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 In carry trades, investors borrow in a low-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to invest in one with higher returns.\par \par \uc2 Another reason the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been strong and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely need to continue is the strength of the very exporters Japanese authorities are seeking to help through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Busch said. "This will continue and therefore their need to sell U.S. dollars on any rally will remain," he said.\par \par \uc2 Purchasing of Japanese Government Bonds by foreign central banks and reserve managers is another flow arguing for yen strength, said \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's Lynch.\par \par \uc2 The magnitude and intensity of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will depend on its precise objectives. As their intent is likely to curb further yen appreciation rather than push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  significantly lower, further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be relatively contained, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 "Their intention is not to push the dollar above Y90 or Y95 but just to [prevent] the yen from appreciating further," he said.\par \par \uc2 Improvement in U.S. economic data toward the end of the year could bolster the dollar and reduce the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said. But the situation remains unsettled, and if the U.S. economy continues to struggle, the yen could benefit from safe-haven flows, increasing the need for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he added.\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 09-15-10 1429ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000d4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
