\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Tighter 1-Yr Eurodollar Spread Sign Of Slow Economy\b0\par\par\uc2 1,139 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 18:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1613 GMT [Dow Jones] Nearest 1-yr calendar spread is substantially tighter in Eurodollar futures compared to late last week, signaling market fears of a slowing economy and expectations for short-term rates to stay very low for a long time. Dec 2011 contract recently priced in a rate only 41.5 BP above the rate priced into the Dec 2010 contract. Same spread at 42.5 BP Tues, 48.5 BP Mon, and 52 BP Fri. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1551 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options were lower in NY Wed from Tokyo after the dollar leaped against the yen following the \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimated Y1 trillion direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One-month at-the-money USD/JPY options implied volatilities were at 11.80%/12.50%, from 12.45%/13.15% in Tokyo and from 11.85%/12.55% Tue in NY. One-month EUR/USD implied vols were at 10.55%/11.55%, from 10.65%/11.65% in Tokyo and from 10.50%/11.50% Tue in NY. One-month EUR/JPY implied vols were at 13.40%/15.40% from 13.55%/15.55% in Tokyo and from 12.80%/14.80% in NY Tue. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1552 GMT [Dow Jones] The truth is that a very large percentage of Americans consumers is either incredibly scared or totally broke or both, says the Business Insider, with 20 signs the consumer is tapped out. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.businessinsider.com/20-signs-the-american-consumer-is-completely-and-totally-tapped-out-2010-9?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+TheMoneyGame+%28The+Money+Game%29#ixzz0zc7QMgQH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.businessinsider.com/20-signs-the-american-consumer-is-completely-and-totally-tapped-out-2010-9?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+TheMoneyGame+%28The+Money+Game%29#ixzz0zc7QMgQH }}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1532 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 AmeriCredit\uc2  Auto Receivables Trust's $850M ABS, which was increased from $700M, has launched according to a person familiar with the matter. Joint lead managers on the bond, scheduled to price later Wed, are Barc, \uc2 UBS\uc2  and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 . Price guidance is also out: the largest tranche valued at $281M, has launched at 35 bps over over EDSF, a benchmark. Price guidance was in the range of 37 to 40 bps over EDSF. (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1522 GMT [Dow Jones] Former Fed chief Alan Greenspan got a chance to air his views at the Council on Foreign Relations today. The man who spent large stretches of his professional life in public service, and who then was handsomely paid to talk about the insights he gained during that time once out of office, continues to see government as one of the economy's problems. "We have to find a way to simmer down the extent of activism that is going on" with government stimulus spending "and allow the economy to heal" itself, he says. At this point, "we'd probably be better off doing less than more" because "you'd be far better off to allow the normal market forces to operate here," Greenspan said. (michael.derby@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1516 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys maturing in 10 yrs or less are gaining after Fed buys 3- to 7-yr sector. Fed bought $3.89B in notes maturing between Sept 2014 and June 2016. The long bond is the only maturity trading lower but it has pared a big chunk of early losses. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1514 GMT [Dow Jones] Repeat & correct: No evidence of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. market in the face of emerging scuttle that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already acted in N.Y. trading hours, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York. Investors are looking for follow-up to roughly Y1 trillion of yen-selling in Asia and Europe so far. (Corrects to fix spelling of firm name in headline at 1505 GMT) (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1509 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed buys $3.89B in nine Treasurys notes with maturities spanning between Sept. 30, 2014 and June 30, 2016. Dealers submit $16.628 billion bids. The Fed started small-scale buying in Treasurys last month, using proceeds from its MBS paydowns as a way to push down long-term interest rates to support a slowing economy. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1505 GMT [Dow Jones] No evidence of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in U.S. market in the face of emerging scuttle that Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already acted in N.Y. trading hours, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York. Investors are looking for follow-up to roughly Y1 trillion of yen-selling in Asia and Europe so far. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1501 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira is stronger as EUR gains ground against USD. USD/TRY trades at 1.4905, from 1.4950 Tuesday. Meanwhile bonds are weaker after ratings agency S&P Wednesday reaffirmed that Turkey's sovereign rating is unlikely to be upgraded imminently, even as it revised the country's growth forecast upward and praised its robust economic recovery. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond ends trades at 8.09% from 8.04% Tuesday and it moves to 8.09% in Thursday value-dated transactions. Analysts forecast TRY will trade between 1.4890 and 1.5010 and the benchmark bond between 8.00% and 8.15%, in the short term. (yeliz.candemir@iha.com.tr)\par \par \uc2 1456 GMT [Dow Jones] The increase in Canadian home sales was mostly in B.C. (up 2.4%) and Ontario (up 5.6%), likely because of a reversal of the decline in sales that coincided with a tax change in both provinces. Home buyers there rushed to buy ahead of the tax implementation to avoid higher financial and legal costs. Canada's existing home sales rose by a seasonally adjusted 0.4% in August, departing from a declining trend that characterized most of this year. Year-over-year, sales were down 22.5%, a marginal improvement from July's 24.9% year-over-year slip. (karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1452 GMT [Dow Jones] Flows into muni funds continue to be strong, and there's still lots of demand for bond offerings, says Dan Solender at Lord Abbett. For example, a $135M tax-exempt deal Tue for CA's Torrance Memorial Medical Center was oversubscribed at least a couple of times and saw yields lowered, he notes. On Wed, there's lots of offerings in the $50M-$100M range as well as a roughly $639M competitive deal from Washington state as munis prices steady, despite Treasury volatility, Solender says. (kelly.nolan@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 1213ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000cf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
