\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Harrisburg Makes $3.29M GO Bond Payment\b0\par\par\uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 15:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1356 GMT [Dow Jones] Harrisburg, PA is making its $3.29M GO bond payment as due Wed., according to spokesman Chuck Ardo. The city was going to skip the payment, which would have been the second-largest GO default this year, until the state stepped in with $4.4M in expedited funds and grants over the weekend. (romy.varghese@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1351 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed is set to buy 4-6 year Treasurys at 10:30 a.m. EDT, giving support to this part of the curve. Long end lower, though the 10-yr note has recouped most of its losses. Five-year up 3/32 to yield 1.422%; 10-year off 2/32 to yield 2.690%. The 30-year bond is 24/32 lower to yield 3.844%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1345 GMT [Dow Jones] Peru President Alan Garcia's decision to appoint a new prime minister, Jose Chang, and a new finance minister, Ismael Benavides, won't likely lead to any policy changes, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  said. "While the change in leadership in the Finance Ministry was a surprise, we do not think that this should be negative news for the market because the appointment of a new finance minister is unlikely to change the country's economic policies." Peru's market-friendly economic policies are expected to help its economy expand by about 8.0% this year. \uc2 UBS\uc2  said it expects GDP to have expanded by 10.4% in July. (robert.kozak@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1344 GMT [Dow Jones] Asset-backed issuers follow same pattern as corporate issuers as spreads tighten on launching. Auto-maker \uc2 BMW\uc2  increases deal to $1B from $750M and has launched it, according to a person familiar with the matter. The largest tranche valued at $363M, has launched at 25 bps over EDSF, vs 30 bp guidance. The $350M tranche, launched at 15 bps over the same benchmark vs 20-25 bp guidance. The bond, led by Citi and JPM, is scheduled to price on Wed.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (anusha.shrivastava@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1339 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures prices a little lower, reflecting slightly higher short-term yields, staying in range established before data. Market grapples with impact of yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and rare increase in 3-month dollar Libor. Quarterly 2011-through-2013 Eurodollar contracts down 0.5 to 2.5 BP. Nearby Dec '10 -- closely tracking Libor -0.5 BP higher, seeing 0.395% rate at Dec. 13 contract expiration. Cash Libor at 0.29203% at Wed's fixing. (howard.packowitz@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1337 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan appears to have drawn a line in the sand at Y82, says Kathy Lien of GFT \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might have scared off enough investors to hold that line, she says. "Although it is often said that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  never works, especially when it is solo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , in the case of USD/JPY, we believe that the Japanese government has done enough to carve out a temporary bottom in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair," Lien says. The Japanese government has effectively warned speculators that the yen is no longer a one-way trade, she says. USD/JPY up strongly, at 85.50 from 83.08 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG, though pair has come off slightly from its intraday high of 85.78 seen in early NY trading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (bradley.davis@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1337 GMT [Dow Jones] Polish Treasury bonds are stronger following the successful 10-year T-Bond auction, with demand three times more than supply. But the top-up tender has already triggered some profit taking, traders say. Average yield on 2-year bonds is flat at 4.65%, on 5-year at 5.11% from 5.14% late Tuesday, on 10-year at 5.46% from 5.50%. (malgorzata.halaba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1326 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys recover further ground from the early selling after disappointing industrial production report, which showed a 0.2% gain, lower than 0.3% forecast. Bonds had started to bounce off the session lows after the Empire State manufacturing report and in front of Fed purchase of 4-6 yr notes. 10-year note 4/32 lower to yield 2.697%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1321 GMT [Dow Jones] Total manufacturing output was up 0.2% in August, but weakness came in autos, probably a function of automakers staying open in July instead of closing down as usual. Production of motor vehicles and parts plunged 5.2% after 8.3% gain in July. Excluding autos, production in other parts of the economy expanded 0.4% following a revised 0.3% increase in July. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors had little reaction to slighter weaker than expected U.S. industrial production numbers reported for Aug. USD/JPY holding strong at 85.62, from 83.08 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY hovering around its intra-day high of 85.78 leading into data release. Pair has jumped more than 3% on heels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as U.S. investors await next move in Wed session. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1318 GMT [Dow Jones] Another disappointing--but not disastrous--factory report today: Industrial production increased 0.2% in August, less than 0.3% gain expected. July's production figures were revised down to a 0.6% gain from the 1.0% rise initially reported. Capacity utilization also increased, climbing slightly to 74.7% from a revised 74.6% in July. A rate of 75.0% was expected. The industrial production comes out less than an hour after the New York Fed reported its Empire State index fell to 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1316 GMT [Dow Jones] Corporate issuance is off to a slower start Wednesday than earlier this week. Among new offerings, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Harsco Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  is in the market with $250 mln in 5-yr notes via joint books Citi and RBS. Proceeds will repay debt, including borrowings under its U.S. and euro commercial paper programs and its 7.25% British pound sterling-denominated notes, as well as and for other general corporate purposes. Ratings BAA1/A-. Pricing today. (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0956ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
