laba@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1326 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys recover further ground from the early selling after disappointing industrial production report, which showed a 0.2% gain, lower than 0.3% forecast. Bonds had started to bounce off the session lows after the Empire State manufacturing report and in front of Fed purchase of 4-6 yr notes. 10-year note 4/32 lower to yield 2.697%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1321 GMT [Dow Jones] Total manufacturing output was up 0.2% in August, but weakness came in autos, probably a function of automakers staying open in July instead of closing down as usual. Production of motor vehicles and parts plunged 5.2% after 8.3% gain in July. Excluding autos, production in other parts of the economy expanded 0.4% following a revised 0.3% increase in July. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors had little reaction to slighter weaker than expected U.S. industrial production numbers reported for Aug. USD/JPY holding strong at 85.62, from 83.08 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. USD/JPY hovering around its intra-day high of 85.78 leading into data release. Pair has jumped more than 3% on heels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as U.S. investors await next move in Wed session. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1318 GMT [Dow Jones] Another disappointing--but not disastrous--factory report today: Industrial production increased 0.2% in August, less than 0.3% gain expected. July's production figures were revised down to a 0.6% gain from the 1.0% rise initially reported. Capacity utilization also increased, climbing slightly to 74.7% from a revised 74.6% in July. A rate of 75.0% was expected. The industrial production comes out less than an hour after the New York Fed reported its Empire State index fell to 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1316 GMT [Dow Jones] Corporate issuance is off to a slower start Wednesday than earlier this week. Among new offerings, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Harsco Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  is in the market with $250 mln in 5-yr notes via joint books Citi and RBS. Proceeds will repay debt, including borrowings under its U.S. and euro commercial paper programs and its 7.25% British pound sterling-denominated notes, as well as and for other general corporate purposes. Ratings BAA1/A-. Pricing today. (michael.aneiro@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1313 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market creates winners and losers in Treasurys. The winners are those short-dated maturities, like T-bills, as dollar proceeds from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are recycled. Yet longer-dated maturities, especially the 10-yr and 30-yr, are losing ground as Japanese investors book profit. Hedge-related selling related to a complex financial instrument, known as power reverse dual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  notes, also weighs, though the losses are capped in after a soft empire state manufacturing report. 10-year 8/32 lower to yield 2.710%. 3-month T-bill yld down about 1 bp to 0.147%.(min.zeng@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0956ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000aq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
