\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: T-Bills Benefit From  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,081 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 15:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1313 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market creates winners and losers in Treasurys. The winners are those short-dated maturities, like T-bills, as dollar proceeds from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are recycled. Yet longer-dated maturities, especially the 10-yr and 30-yr, are losing ground as Japanese investors book profit. Hedge-related selling related to a complex financial instrument, known as power reverse dual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  notes, also weighs, though the losses are capped in after a soft empire state manufacturing report. 10-year 8/32 lower to yield 2.710%. 3-month T-bill yld down about 1 bp to 0.147%.(min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1303 GMT [Dow Jones] The ruble is falling against the dollar-euro basket as local and international traders cut their oil-fueled long positions on the Russian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after being spooked by the massive local demand for dollars, VTB Capital says. Russian corporate treasurers, remembering ruble weakness late in 2009, are moving into dollars early ahead of upcoming foreign debt repayments, analyst Mikhail Anisimov says. Ruble slips to 35.12 against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 1259 GMT, from 34.72 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed at 30.81 against the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1302 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for first time in more than six years has immediately had desired effect: "scaring \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders away from trying to push the yen higher," notes LA Times' Tom Petruno. "Despite its aging population and huge government debt load, Japan and the yen have been viewed by some foreign investors as safer havens than the US in the aftermath of the 2008 financial crisis," Petruno says, noting very few countries want a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  these days. "The weaker your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the cheaper your exports get for foreign buyers. In a world glutted with unused industrial capacity and with labor, everyone's looking for someone to buy their stuff." (steven.russolillo@dowjones.com) ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/money_co/2010/09/japan-weakens-yen-dollar-currency-exporters-kan.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://latimesblogs.latimes.com/money_co/2010/09/japan-weakens-yen-dollar-\b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 -exporters-kan.html}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 1301 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD notches a modest decline vs USD as weak data from both countries send investors seeking USD safety. In Canada, July manufacturing sales declined more than expected, marking a 0.9% slip. The market had expected a 0.2% increase. In the U.S., import prices showed a 0.6% rise in Aug from July. NY Fed manufacturing activity showed expansion in September, but at a slower pace than in August. USD was at 1.0306, from 1.0283 ahead of the data. It was at 1.0243 late Tues, according to EBS via CQG. karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1252 GMT [Dow Jones] The Russian ruble may soon be lifted from its current 7-month low against the dollar-euro basket by corporate tax payments due at the end of the month, RBC says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be trading at 34.2 against the basket, rather than its current price of 34.9, but has recently decoupled from the moves in crude prices, the investment bank says. (ira.iosebashvili@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1248 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY weakened slightly but still up strongly on day after weaker than expected new Empire State manufacturing index. USD/JPY at 85.56, from 83.08 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. Pair has still soared more than 3% on heels of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. export prices resumed an upward trend in August, increasing 0.8% after declining the two previous months. The August increase was the largest monthly advance since a 1.1% rise in April. Export prices of agricultural goods and industrial supplies contributed to the rise. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are off lows after the NY Fed Empire State Index fell more than forecast in September. The business conditions index fell to 4.14 vs 7.10 in August, and expectations are for a reading of 7.0. U.S. industrial production data follow at 1315 GMT. December bunds are down 0.40 at 130.05. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] The bigger problem with Wednesday's yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the signal it sends to the world, says the Curious Capitalist. "That message is: We want to export to you, not vice versa, for the good of our own recovery, not yours... The fact is that not every country can export its way out of the Great Recession." (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)(\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://curiouscapitalist.blogs.time.com/2010/09/15/why-japan%e2%80%99s-yen-policy-is-bad-for-the-world/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+timeblogs%2Fcurious_capitalist+%28TIME%3A+The+Curious+Capitalist%29#ixzz0zbJQjjSQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://curiouscapitalist.blogs.time.com/2010/09/15/why-japan%e2%80%99s-yen-policy-is-bad-for-the-world/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+timeblogs%2Fcurious_capitalist+%28TIME%3A+The+Curious+Capitalist%29#ixzz0zbJQjjSQ}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Moscow (MMBM.RS) bonds fall after state media portray Mayor Yuri Luzhkov in a negative light, sparking speculation the Kremlin may oust him. The price of the bank's bonds due 2105 fell markedly, pushing the yield to 6.18%, VTB Capital says. "It would appear that the slew of negative reports, both on TV and in print, about the activities of Moscow's Mayor Yuri Luzhkov were the main reason for this," analyst Maxim Korovin explains. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD was little changed after July manufacturing sales declined more than expected, notching a 0.9% slip. The market had expected a 0.2% increase. USD was at 1.0290, from 1.0283 ahead of the data. It was at 1.0243 late Tues, according to EBS via CQG. karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1235 GMT [Dow Jones] Within the August 0.6% rise in total import prices, fuel prices gained 1.7%. The rise was led by a 2.1% increase in petroleum prices -- the largest advance since April -- which more than offset a 3.5% decline in natural gas prices. Excluding petroleum, import prices were 0.2% higher in August. Food import prices were up 2.2% in August, the largest one-month increase since March 2008. Rising prices for vegetables, meat and food oils all contributed to the increase. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0916ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f000a3\par }\page {\par\fs20{