\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Dips Slightly On Empire Data\b0\par\par\uc2 1,039 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 14:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1248 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY weakened slightly but still up strongly on day after weaker than expected new Empire State manufacturing index. USD/JPY at 85.56, from 83.08 late Tue, according to EBS via CQG. Pair has still soared more than 3% on heels of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1240 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. export prices resumed an upward trend in August, increasing 0.8% after declining the two previous months. The August increase was the largest monthly advance since a 1.1% rise in April. Export prices of agricultural goods and industrial supplies contributed to the rise. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1238 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are off lows after the NY Fed Empire State Index fell more than forecast in September. The business conditions index fell to 4.14 vs 7.10 in August, and expectations are for a reading of 7.0. U.S. industrial production data follow at 1315 GMT. December bunds are down 0.40 at 130.05. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1237 GMT [Dow Jones] The bigger problem with Wednesday's yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is the signal it sends to the world, says the Curious Capitalist. "That message is: We want to export to you, not vice versa, for the good of our own recovery, not yours... The fact is that not every country can export its way out of the Great Recession." (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)(\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://curiouscapitalist.blogs.time.com/2010/09/15/why-japan%e2%80%99s-yen-policy-is-bad-for-the-world/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+timeblogs%2Fcurious_capitalist+%28TIME%3A+The+Curious+Capitalist%29#ixzz0zbJQjjSQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://curiouscapitalist.blogs.time.com/2010/09/15/why-japan%e2%80%99s-yen-policy-is-bad-for-the-world/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+timeblogs%2Fcurious_capitalist+%28TIME%3A+The+Curious+Capitalist%29#ixzz0zbJQjjSQ}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] Bank of Moscow (MMBM.RS) bonds fall after state media portray Mayor Yuri Luzhkov in a negative light, sparking speculation the Kremlin may oust him. The price of the bank's bonds due 2105 fell markedly, pushing the yield to 6.18%, VTB Capital says. "It would appear that the slew of negative reports, both on TV and in print, about the activities of Moscow's Mayor Yuri Luzhkov were the main reason for this," analyst Maxim Korovin explains. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] CAD was little changed after July manufacturing sales declined more than expected, notching a 0.9% slip. The market had expected a 0.2% increase. USD was at 1.0290, from 1.0283 ahead of the data. It was at 1.0243 late Tues, according to EBS via CQG. karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1235 GMT [Dow Jones] Within the August 0.6% rise in total import prices, fuel prices gained 1.7%. The rise was led by a 2.1% increase in petroleum prices -- the largest advance since April -- which more than offset a 3.5% decline in natural gas prices. Excluding petroleum, import prices were 0.2% higher in August. Food import prices were up 2.2% in August, the largest one-month increase since March 2008. Rising prices for vegetables, meat and food oils all contributed to the increase. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1233 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. import prices rose more than expected in August, thanks to higher energy and food prices, a move that should help quell fears that the economy may fall into deflation. The price of goods imported into the U.S. rose 0.6% last month, following a 0.1% rise in July, revised from a previously reported 0.2% increase. Economists were expecting import prices to increase 0.3% in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1230 GMT [Dow Jones] New York manufacturing activity is expanding this month, but at a slower pace than in August, according to the New York Fed's Empire State Manufacturing Survey. The business conditions index fell to 4.14 in September from 7.10 in August, worse than 7.0 expected reading. The employment index remained positive, but little changed, standing at 14.93 in September from 14.29. (kathleen.madigan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits 85.67, up about 3% from 83.08 late Tue, on back of first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in six years. Market awaits next move by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "MOF likely to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in NY," to boost its chances of eventual success, said Steven Englander, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Citi. After \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during Asian and European trading, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prepared to take action again in the US market if needed, a Japanese government official said Wed. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1225 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD at 1.2978, down from 1.3020 late Tue, ahead of Empire State manufacturing index due at 8:30am ET. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  the talk of market ahead of the domestic data marker. USD/JPY at 85.52, from 83.08, according to EBS via CQG. GBP/USD at 1.5540, from 1.5563. Consensus for Sep index at 5.0, after 7.1 result for Aug.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1222 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys fall for the first time this week as many investors booked profit following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. The move fuels strong rally in the Japanese stock market, prompting shift from Treasurys into stocks. The sharp fall in the yen versus the dollar also sparked hedging-related selling in Treasurys as many traders hedge bets on a complex financial instrument whose value linked with the dollar/yen, overshadowing MOF's buying of the dollar, whose proceeds are usually parked in Treasurys. Up next is Empire State manufacturing report and import prices data at 8:30 a.m. ET. The Fed will buy Treasurys at 10:30 a.m. ET, targeting maturities between September 30, 2014 and August 31, 2016. 10-year note's price is 11/32 lower to yield 2.721%. The 30-year bond is 1 2/32 lower to yield 3.861%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0848ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0009k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
