\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY At Session High; Eyes On Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 1,525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 14:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in New York.  Darlene Ross, 212 416-2166; \par darlene.ross@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1226 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY hits 85.67, up about 3% from 83.08 late Tue, on back of first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in six years. Market awaits next move by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . "MOF likely to continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in NY," to boost its chances of eventual success, said Steven Englander, head of G10 \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Citi. After \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during Asian and European trading, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prepared to take action again in the US market if needed, a Japanese government official said Wed. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1225 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD at 1.2978, down from 1.3020 late Tue, ahead of Empire State manufacturing index due at 8:30am ET. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the talk of market ahead of the domestic data marker. USD/JPY at 85.52, from 83.08, according to EBS via CQG. GBP/USD at 1.5540, from 1.5563. Consensus for Sep index at 5.0, after 7.1 result for Aug.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1222 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys fall for the first time this week as many investors booked profit following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the yen. The move fuels strong rally in the Japanese stock market, prompting shift from Treasurys into stocks. The sharp fall in the yen versus the dollar also sparked hedging-related selling in Treasurys as many traders hedge bets on a complex financial instrument whose value linked with the dollar/yen, overshadowing MOF's buying of the dollar, whose proceeds are usually parked in Treasurys. Up next is Empire State manufacturing report and import prices data at 8:30 a.m. ET. The Fed will buy Treasurys at 10:30 a.m. ET, targeting maturities between September 30, 2014 and August 31, 2016. 10-year note's price is 11/32 lower to yield 2.721%. The 30-year bond is 1 2/32 lower to yield 3.861%. (min.zeng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [Dow Jones] Homeowners who could refinance have already started the process, leading the MBA refi index to fall 10.8% this week. The 35 BP rise in Treasury rates over the last month didn't help, although mortgage rates did not fluctuate that much since homeowners were never offered rates at rock bottom levels. 30-yr rates at 4.47%, origination fees continue to kick up (now 1.08 pts) and capacity has probably been reached. Purchase index was down 0.4%. (darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1208 GMT [Dow Jones] EUROPEAN SUMMARY: The BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets Wednesday for the first time since March 2006 reportedly spending as much as Y1Tln to halt USD/JPY's slide below Y83.00. The spot immediately added around one yen from its fresh 15-year low of Y82.87 and climbed to a high of 85.54 in Europe while EUR/JPY peaked at a one-month high of 111.11. Dips in USD/JPY were limited to around 85.00 after an MOF official let it be known that further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had taken place during the European session. So far the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been unsterilized, which means it increases money supply and is effectively a form of monetary easing. The BOJ's surprise move helped the dollar recovery elsewhere after Tuesday's sharp sell-off triggered by speculation that the Fed will ease monetary policy again before year end. EUR/USD slipped from 1.3024 to 1.2955, USD/CHF and EUR/CHF added one cent to 1.0049 and 1.3048, respectively, amid speculation that the BOJ's action could trigger a response from the SNB ahead of Thursday rate set meeting. GBP/USD took a small hit after the UK August claimant count unexpectedly rose by 2.3k instead of falling by 4k. However, late session had fully recovered to print the day's high of 1.5569. Elsewhere, gold traded $2 under Tuesday's fresh all-time high of $1274.75oz and the main European bourses are between 0.2% and 0.4% lower. European government bonds are lower with some traders citing the BOJ's unsterilized \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a factor. December bunds are currently down 0.25 at 130.26 while December gilts are 0.32 lower at 122.90 not helped by a lackluster auction of 20-year gilts. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY at 85.43, from 83.08 late Tue, on back of first \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in six years, selling about 1 trillion JPY during Asian and European hours to hinder the yen's appreciation. Investors will be watching closely for any signs of Japan's MOF once US trading become active. Japan prepared to take action again in the US market if needed, a Japanese government official said Wed. EUR/JPY climbs to 110.58 from 108.20, according to EBS via CQG. EUR/USD slightly off at 1.2987 from 1.3020 late Tue, while GBP/USD at 1.5550 from 1.5563. (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1155 GMT [Dow Jones] While some of Treasury strength Tues. was attributed to reports there would be another announcement of Fed buying, Richmond Fed Pres Lacker tells WSJ the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only do more if it was looking at a real threat of consumer price deflation, something he doesn't see. "For me if the growth numbers come in about where the consensus forecast is and we continue to get inflation between 1% and 2%, I don't believe I would see a need for further stimulus. Others may disagree but that's how I see it," Lacker said. Treasurys weak early, despite short end support after yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .(darlene.ross@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (\f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2010/09/14/richmond-feds-lacker-wants-high-threshold-for-more-fed-action/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+wsj%2Feconomics%2Ffeed+%28WSJ.com%3A+Real+Time+Economics+Blog%29"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/economics/2010/09/14/richmond-feds-lacker-wants-high-threshold-for-more-fed-action/?utm_source=feedburner&utm_medium=feed&utm_campaign=Feed%3A+wsj%2Feconomics%2Ffeed+%28WSJ.com%3A+Real+Time+Economics+Blog%29}}}\uc2 \f2 )\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1202 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  likes Hungarian interest rate risk but doesn't like credit risk. Expects the government bond yield curve to flatten, with the front-end rising if credit risk premium increases even more ahead of October's local elections. Regards the long end as "quite attractive from a longer-term perspective." Hungarian yields are among the highest in emerging markets, and given that inflation is likely to decline toward 3%, this implies an expected 10-year real yield of about 4% over the next 12 months, "which is not bad at all." But remains more cautious in the near term mainly because the credit risk premium may also go even higher, keeping longer yields elevated. Holds a 2s/10s flattener in Hungary. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1201 GMT [Dow Jones] Fears about economies turning Japanese in the wake of the financial crisis miss on two counts, argues Wednesday's Money Talks column. On the one hand, bad demographics make Japan's lost decade worse than it was. But on the other, Japan faces a massive disaster when the government's huge deficits can only be covered by debt monetization. Which is likely to happen sooner than many people think. (alen.mattich@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1140 GMT [Dow Jones] News that Greece plans to issue 'diaspora bonds' in 2011 meets little enthusiasm. "This will not be a game changer for the GGB market and because the Greek diaspora is likely to be a leveraged consumer, as opposed to the previous generation of large savers, they are unlikely to pull in huge funds," says a trader. "These offers do not come close to providing the sizeable amounts Greece will need from 2012 onwards to fund its deficits and national debt. They will be more symbolic than anything else." (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1125 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP comes under heavy pressure as GBP/USD adds almost half a cent to 1.5550. The cross now trades at the day's low of 0.8345 with next support seen between 0.8325 and 0.83 says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1119 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore exports likely continued to grow at double-digit rate in August, though at slower pace than previous months as base effect diminishes. Non-oil domestic exports expected +14.9% on-year vs +18.2% in July, Dow Jones Newswires poll of 6 economists shows. Compared with previous month, August exports expected to decline 1.5% in seasonally adjusted terms. Exports shrank 3.9% on-month in July. Possible weakness in pharmaceuticals sector could contribute to export softness. "While we will still continue to expect healthy year-on-year growth of headline NODX due to the favourable low base effect...this effect will be much weaker in 2H compared to 1H," Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says. Data due 0500 GMT Friday. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0827ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0009a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
