\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF Often Driven By German/Greek Yield Spread\b0\par\par\uc2 1,317 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 13:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1102 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF is often driven by changes in the German/Greek government bond yield spread, according to ErsteGroup. "Lately, an increase in the spread, reflecting doubts on southern European government debt and the corresponding risk aversion, was often accompanied by a strengthening of the franc," notes analyst Mildred Hager. "This points to safe haven flows that might be less related to equity markets than worries about government debt." EUR/CHF at 1.3002 by 1100 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 1059 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds off Highs as traders adjust positions ahead of RBI's monetary policy review Thursday, says state-run bank trader. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 98.97 after climbing to 99.27 in session, 99.01 last close. "A 25 basis point hike in both rates has already been priced in so the markets may see a mild rally if RBI moves according to expectations. I think the growth-inflation picture is largely manageable and RBI may not be too hawkish as inflation is showing signs of tapering off," trader says. Adds, bonds will be boosted if RBI narrows interest rate corridor by only hiking key borrowing rate. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 98.95-99.10 band for rest of session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1046 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY peaked at 85.54 in Europe Wednesday and is now slipping toward the session low around 85.00. One London bank trader says the BOJ was reportedly still buying USD/JPY around 84.70 in the Asia session so he would caution against holding short positions much beyond current levels. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1045 GMT [Dow Jones] The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have achieved its short-term objective by prompting a strong rally in USD/JPY, but the lesson that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rarely works over the longer-term has once again been overlooked. The prevailing long-term bear trend is likely to retest the 82.87 reaction low in the not-too-distant future, leaving the April 1995 all-time low at 79.92 vulnerable. See Wednesday's Charting Europe column by Francis Bray. (francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 GMT [Dow Jones] Swiss economic research institute BAK Basel raises its forecast for 2010 Swiss gross domestic product growth to 2.7%, from 1.9%, after particularly robust expansion in 1H. "The pace of growth will ease to 1.4% next year, a development seen as normal after a strong recovery, rather than an economic slump," says economist Alexis Bill Koerber. "The appreciation of the franc versus the euro and weaker global demand will slow the growth in exports." (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1036 GMT [Dow Jones] A credit rating downgrade of Hungary to junk category looms, \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  economist Timothy Ash says. Notes markets and rating agency Standard & Poors are giving Hungary the benefit of the doubt until the municipal elections October 3 but, after that, they will want to see either Hungary's recommitment to the IMF/EU program or "real commitment" to a medium-term fiscal program. Says: "It makes you wonder whether it will need a rating downgrade really to concentrate politicians' minds [on those issues]." Adds that the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be "pulling in the same direction, which doesn't seem to be the case at the moment." (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1033 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx indexes turn red after weak Portuguese T-bill sale and UK unemployment numbers. Europe index 1 bp wider at 105.5/106.5 bps and Crossover 3 bps weaker at 473/477 bps. The widening reverses early gains which came on the back on BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Primary markets are tick over with KPN, the European Union and Hessen all raising new money. (michael.wilson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1032 GMT [Dow Jones] India's RBI may keep key policy rates unchanged at Thursday's policy review "to take stock of its earlier rate hikes" as monetary policy impacts inflation with a lag, ICICI Bank says. Adds, most banks raising their lending and term deposit rates gives reason for RBI to evaluate impact before resuming its normalization path. Reckons broad-based easing in inflationary pressures, with the exception of food inflation, and expected slowdown in capital goods growth may also result in pause in rate hikes Thursday. "The timing of the rate hike is also important. In the current scenario, liquidity is expected to remain tight till the November policy meeting as around INR450 billion is expected to move out of the (banking) system due to advance tax payments," house says. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1031 GMT [Dow Jones] Greece/10-year bund spread holds steady at 900 bps while turnover is modest at EUR20M on the local HDAT and European trading platform. "The Greek Finance Minister's investor roadshows in London, Paris and Frankfurt hold some promise to turn around the negative sentiment on the Greek bond market," says a senior local bond trader. "It won't cure all the markets problems and the mistrust but it could encourage some to rethink their positions," he says. "I will be watching to see if real money accounts decide in the next few days that there is value in GGBs," he adds. (nick.skrekas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1030 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's convoluted game of wordplay spiraled to end Wednesday when Japanese government moved from verbal to actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after months of speculation. Earlier this month, Noda said Japanese authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength; he also repeated his pledge to take "decisive steps" in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when it becomes necessary. Today, Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for first time in more than 6 years to prevent sharp rise in JPY from dragging on country's export-reliant economy. Japanese authorities started buying USD, selling JPY soon after USD/JPY hit new 15-year low of 82.87 at around 0130 GMT. Speaking at news conference after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps were announced: Noda made clear that more action could be coming, saying "we will continue to closely monitor movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and will take decisive steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when necessary." See: =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Noda's Countdown To Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (yoree.koh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] The signs are looking good for GBP/USD says Credit Agricole. It has broken and closed above its 20 DMA at 1.5457, daily MACDs have turned higher from oversold levels and the spot appears to be confirming earlier signs of positive divergence. Take all this together and the bank says it favors buying GBP/USD at market with an intraday stop at 1.5305 for a target of the August 1.5905 high. GBP/USD now at 1.5502. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT [Dow Jones] If Vivendi's (VIV.FR) buyout of \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 's (VOD) 44% stake in SFR materialises, it should provide an opportunity to go short the name, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , as the company would need to draw on credit facility to fund the acquisition which would likely cause some widening in the company's CDS spreads. "We [currently] believe Vivendi's 5-year CDS offers little upside in terms of spread tightening relative to the group's fundamental credit profile," says BNP's Hunter Martin. "As a result, we would advocate taking a short-position in Vivendi via 5Y CDS - potentially in a pair trade versus the Main - with limited downside, from the potential widening in the event that SFR rumours come to fruition." (michael.wilson@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0702ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0008a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
