\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Noda's Countdown To JPY  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  - WSJ BLOG\b0\par\par\uc2 1,405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 12:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's convoluted game of wordplay spiraled to end Wednesday when Japanese government moved from verbal to actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after months of speculation. Earlier this month, Noda said Japanese authorities "are conducting various simulations" to curb the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength; he also repeated his pledge to take "decisive steps" in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market when it becomes necessary. Today, Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for first time in more than 6 years to prevent sharp rise in JPY from dragging on country's export-reliant economy. Japanese authorities started buying USD, selling JPY soon after USD/JPY hit new 15-year low of 82.87 at around 0130 GMT. Speaking at news conference after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  steps were announced: Noda made clear that more action could be coming, saying "we will continue to closely monitor movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and will take decisive steps, including \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , when necessary." See: =WSJ BLOG/\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Time: Noda's Countdown To Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (yoree.koh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 GMT [Dow Jones] The signs are looking good for GBP/USD says Credit Agricole. It has broken and closed above its 20 DMA at 1.5457, daily MACDs have turned higher from oversold levels and the spot appears to be confirming earlier signs of positive divergence. Take all this together and the bank says it favors buying GBP/USD at market with an intraday stop at 1.5305 for a target of the August 1.5905 high. GBP/USD now at 1.5502. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1017 GMT [Dow Jones] If Vivendi's (VIV.FR) buyout of \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 's (VOD) 44% stake in SFR materialises, it should provide an opportunity to go short the name, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 , as the company would need to draw on credit facility to fund the acquisition which would likely cause some widening in the company's CDS spreads. "We [currently] believe Vivendi's 5-year CDS offers little upside in terms of spread tightening relative to the group's fundamental credit profile," says BNP's Hunter Martin. "As a result, we would advocate taking a short-position in Vivendi via 5Y CDS - potentially in a pair trade versus the Main - with limited downside, from the potential widening in the event that SFR rumours come to fruition." (michael.wilson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1014 GMT [Dow Jones] India interest rate swaps mostly in range but back-end under some pressure ahead of RBI policy meeting Thursday, says trader with foreign bank; 1-year OIS at 6.17%/6.21% vs 6.17%/6.20% last close, 5-year OIS at 6.95%/6.99% vs 6.95%/6.98% last close. Front-end may stay more or less at current levels until rate decision tomorrow, says trader. Back-end though biased down; rapid unwinding of early paid positions today suggests market reluctant to pay at current levels. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1005 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB higher at 30.85 vs 30.73-30.74 late yesterday, tracking broad USD strength vs EUR, regional units. Still, pair off 30.87 intraday high after BOT governor says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no immediate plan to impose measures to curb THB's rise. "The baht was weak earlier in the morning session but it has recovered some ground (after the governor's speech)," says Bangkok-based dealer. BOT not spotted in market today, dealers say. Resistance tipped at 30.90, support at 30.80 Thursday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT [Dow Jones] The Japanese authorities need to be "aggressive now and hit the market hard, fast and furious because as time goes on, the 'announcement [of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] effect' will dissipate," says David Bloom of \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . The authorities may be successful at first, but as time goes on global co-\b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be needed for it to work, he adds. According to BIS, the yen market is worth $740B a day, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  notes. "The amounts become so large they start to work against international acceptance of this policy." USD/JPY at 85.38 from a low of 82.85. (clare.connaghan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1000 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR forward premia down as traders pull back on previous rate-driven bets on growing view RBI's Thursday policy meeting could throw up relatively dovish outcome on policy rates, says trader with state-run bank; 1-year forward premium at 2.06 vs 2.08 late Tuesday. Fate of premia now depends entirely on RBI meet outcome; sharper than 25 bp rate hike may force August contract to rally to 2.40. More likely though, in-line 25 bp hike or maybe even less could trigger correction below 2.00. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end flat at 921.10, recouping earlier losses after BOT Governor Tarisa says it has no immediate plans to impose measures to stem THB rise. "The market had clearly priced in the recent good news; booming exports, recovering tourism, 3G licensing and stable politics. And this week the newsflow turned negative with worries over the baht, reds marching, the coup anniversary and increased security on the streets not to mention a market that had got a little too far ahead of itself," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities; adds valuations, however, not stretched with bourse trading below long term average valuation of 13.6X PER, 1.8X PB. Support at 910, resistance at 925. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 0.4% at THB281, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 2.1% at THB92.50, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.3% at THB105, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) down 0.4% at THB118.50, Bangkok Bank (BBL.TH) down 1.7% at THB143. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0956 GMT [Dow Jones] The successful sale of EUR750 million Portuguese 12-month T-bills at an average yield of 3.369% fails to unsettle euro zone peripheral bond yield spreads. The 2-year Portuguese/German spread is 2 bps tighter at 276 bps, the 5-year spread is 1 bp tighter at 314.5 bps and the 10-year spread is 1 bp wider at 335 bps. Across the other peripheral countries, the 10-year Italian/German spread is unchanged at 149 bps, Spanish/German is unchanged at 178 bps, Irish/German is unchanged at 361.5 bps and Greek/German is up 1 bp to 901.5 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0952 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD gains extend beyond 2009 and 2010 peaks at 0.9410 but need a close for the day above that for potential to overthrow its 2008 peak of 0.9850 says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . At present however, with USD/CAD and NZD/USD struggling to push beyond their range extremes, the bank is wary of a temporary stall in AUD/USD, although while that rate is above support at 0.9225, BarCap still favors the topside. AUD/USD now at 0.9367 from the day's fresh 2-year high of 0.9429. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0949 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts slip a tad after demand weakens at an auction of 20-year bonds. The DMO gets a bid-to-cover ratio of 1.49 times at GBP2 Bln sale of UKT 2030, down from 1.86 times at the previous auction held last year. The yield tail also widens to 0.7bp vs 0.5bp previous. The average price of GBP111.00, however, came in above levels in the secondary market prior to the auction. December gilts are down 0.20 at 123.02. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT [Dow Jones] The zloty is up, more against the dollar than the euro. He expects EUR/PLN to break 3.92 Wednesday and at minimum, to remain below 3.96. EUR/PLN now trades at 3.9303 from 3.9376 late Tuesday; USD/PLN at 3.0269 from 3.0626. Average yield on 2-year bonds is at 4.63% from 4.65% late Tuesday, on 5-year at 5.09% from 5.14%, on 10-year at 5.47% from 5.50%. (marynia.kruk@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0630ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0007x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
