\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Peripheral Spreads Stable After Bund Auction\b0\par\par\uc2 1,184 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 11:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0924 GMT [Dow Jones] Euro zone peripheral spreads to German bunds remain stable after the successful 10-year bund auction Wednesday. The German finance agency sold EUR4.395Bln bund 2.25% September 2020 at a cover ratio of 1.8 times and an average yield of 2.39%. The 10-year Italian/German spread is 1bp tighter on the day at 148bps, Spanish/German is unchanged at 178.5bps, Portuguese/German is 1.5bps up at 336bps, Irish/German is unchanged at 361.5bps and Greek/German is up 1.5bps at 902bps, according to Tradeweb. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0923 GMT [Dow Jones] Analysing JPY flows \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  says recent strength has been driven by commercial buying needs and Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserve managers buying JPY denominated 3 and 6 month bills. The bank says commercial JPY buying needs will continue which suggests that USD/JPY will drift lower once the BOJ stops \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY now trades at 85.39. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0922 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares lower, with cement, petroleum shares leading losses. KSE-100 index last down 0.3% at 10,056.10. "There is some fund selling due to uncertainty regarding the new Margin Trading System that is currently being debated," says dealer. Adds, decision on this may come in when SECP Chairman visits stock exchange on September 20. Ahsan Mehanti, analyst at the \uc2 Arif Habib Investment\uc2  says activity will pick up only post this decision. \uc2 Hub Power Co Ltd\uc2  (HPCL.KA) +2.0% at PKR34.00, DGK Cement (DGKC.KA) down 2.1% at PKR26.22. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0912 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are off lows after robust demand at an auction of 10-year bonds. Germany sells EUR4.395 Bln of the September 2020 bund with a bid to cover ratio of 1.8 and an average yield of 2.39%, broadly in line with market levels. December bunds are down 0.18 at 130.33 vs 130.18 session low. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0910 GMT [Dow Jones] What Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  does Wednesday is critical as it is by this that they will be judged, says Bank of New York Mellon. If the MOF's actions are not felt threatening enough then investors will feel comfortable renewing JPY purchases, so the authorities need to make today's move as painful as possible to have the desired effect. BONY says it's worth noting that so far this morning's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has only taken USD/JPY back to where it was at the start of the month and it's possible that many had been waiting for the MOF to act before buying JPY. USD/JPY now at 85.44. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.798 late vs 31.815 yesterday, weighed by foreign fund inflows, record-low USD/CNY fixing, export settlements, says local bank trader. But pair off intraday low of 31.650 as importers buy while \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises concerns Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also introduce tough measures to stem TWD strength. Still, trader says pair biased down, may test 31.600 support Thursday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities steady as spot widely expected to stay in 46.20-47.00 range in near term, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 6.60%/6.80% vs 6.40%/7% late Tuesday. Medium-term bias probably remains towards slow INR rise though, keeping back-end volatilities in-check as well, says trader. 1-year ATMF at 6.50%/7.50%. RBI policy meet Thursday unlikely to throw up surprises, with many expecting 25 bp hike in both key rates. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] Minutes from the Riksbank's latest Monetary Policy Committee meeting show the reason behind the decision to raise the repo rate Sept. 2 was the expectation of rising inflationary pressure as the labor market and the economy gain strength, says Andreas Jonsson, analyst at \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2 . "Keep in mind that after the meeting we have had strong data on the Swedish economy pointing to a stronger growth than Riksbank's forecast. We stick to our view of four consecutive rate hikes," says Jonsson. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2233. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may stay around 85.00 rest of global day--at least that's what Japan officials probably want to happen, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo; adds MOF likely has enough firepower to support pair on its own. "I think (Japanese officials) want to end the day by keeping (dollar/yen above) 85.00 because if they could do that, the view would emerge that the pair has hit bottom. That's what they want to happen." Dealer isn't sure if pair may resume fall if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  halts \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; key is if there will be shift in positions as USD "shorts are being smoked out right now." USD/JPY last at 85.45. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad higher at 8,975 vs 8,965 late yesterday as players cover short positions after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to try to halt JPY's rise vs USD, dealers say; Bank Indonesia not spotted in market today, dealers add. "Capital inflows into Indonesian bond and stock markets put a lid on the greenback," dealer says; 8,965-8,980 range tipped for Thursday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1130 vs yesterday's 3.1040; local dealer says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY raising concerns Bank Negara may take additional measures to curb MYR strength. "The pair almost touched MYR3.1000 but reversed direction pretty swiftly after the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says dealer. Adds, some corporate demand also driving pair higher, while sellers parked at MYR3.1200 will likely keep resistance level firm today. Market will be closed Thursday for public holiday. Dealer tips 3.1050-3.1250 range Friday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] Russian ruble traders are worried as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens to a four-month low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-euro basket despite relatively high oil prices this week. "It's quite unpleasant here, looking like short squeeze a bit, local dealers are nervous," says a ruble trader at a major international investment bank in Moscow. Increased imports hurting the trade balance, acquisitions overseas by Russian companies and the maturity of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt may be weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trader says. Ruble slips to 34.90 against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 0839 GMT, from 34.72 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed at 30.7415 against the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0524ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f00073\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
