\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/TWD Lower; May Test 31.60 Support Thur-Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,104 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 11:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0907 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.798 late vs 31.815 yesterday, weighed by foreign fund inflows, record-low USD/CNY fixing, export settlements, says local bank trader. But pair off intraday low of 31.650 as importers buy while \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raises concerns Taiwan \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may also introduce tough measures to stem TWD strength. Still, trader says pair biased down, may test 31.600 support Thursday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities steady as spot widely expected to stay in 46.20-47.00 range in near term, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 6.60%/6.80% vs 6.40%/7% late Tuesday. Medium-term bias probably remains towards slow INR rise though, keeping back-end volatilities in-check as well, says trader. 1-year ATMF at 6.50%/7.50%. RBI policy meet Thursday unlikely to throw up surprises, with many expecting 25 bp hike in both key rates. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0900 GMT [Dow Jones] Minutes from the Riksbank's latest Monetary Policy Committee meeting show the reason behind the decision to raise the repo rate Sept. 2 was the expectation of rising inflationary pressure as the labor market and the economy gain strength, says Andreas Jonsson, analyst at \uc2 Nordea Bank\uc2 . "Keep in mind that after the meeting we have had strong data on the Swedish economy pointing to a stronger growth than Riksbank's forecast. We stick to our view of four consecutive rate hikes," says Jonsson. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2233. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may stay around 85.00 rest of global day--at least that's what Japan officials probably want to happen, says senior dealer at major European bank in Tokyo; adds MOF likely has enough firepower to support pair on its own. "I think (Japanese officials) want to end the day by keeping (dollar/yen above) 85.00 because if they could do that, the view would emerge that the pair has hit bottom. That's what they want to happen." Dealer isn't sure if pair may resume fall if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  halts \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; key is if there will be shift in positions as USD "shorts are being smoked out right now." USD/JPY last at 85.45. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0854 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR tad higher at 8,975 vs 8,965 late yesterday as players cover short positions after Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to try to halt JPY's rise vs USD, dealers say; Bank Indonesia not spotted in market today, dealers add. "Capital inflows into Indonesian bond and stock markets put a lid on the greenback," dealer says; 8,965-8,980 range tipped for Thursday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1130 vs yesterday's 3.1040; local dealer says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's official \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY raising concerns Bank Negara may take additional measures to curb MYR strength. "The pair almost touched MYR3.1000 but reversed direction pretty swiftly after the yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says dealer. Adds, some corporate demand also driving pair higher, while sellers parked at MYR3.1200 will likely keep resistance level firm today. Market will be closed Thursday for public holiday. Dealer tips 3.1050-3.1250 range Friday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] Russian ruble traders are worried as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakens to a four-month low against the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-euro basket despite relatively high oil prices this week. "It's quite unpleasant here, looking like short squeeze a bit, local dealers are nervous," says a ruble trader at a major international investment bank in Moscow. Increased imports hurting the trade balance, acquisitions overseas by Russian companies and the maturity of foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt may be weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , trader says. Ruble slips to 34.90 against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket at 0839 GMT, from 34.72 late Tuesday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  little changed at 30.7415 against the dollar. (william.mauldin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 GMT [Dow Jones] Treasurys are down in London as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  gives risk sentiment a slight boost. It's "a relief that Japanese authorities are drawing a line in the sand and that's feeding into improved risk sentiment," says a strategist. Wednesday data include Empire State Manufacturing Survey at 1230 GMT and industrial production figures at 1315 GMT. The market will look for evidence of a continuation of recent encouraging data. 2-year note down at 99-24/32 yielding 0.504%, 10-year down at 99-11/32 yielding 2.70%. 30-year down at 100-24+/32, yielding 3.832%. December bunds down 0.23 at 130.28. December gilts 0.23 lower at 122.99. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year gilt/bund yield spread edged lower by 1.5 bps to 67 bps after the UK jobless claimant total unexpectedly rose in August. The spread now trades at 67 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF remains in a well defined stair-step downtrend capped by resistance at 1.3165 notes \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Now at 1.3015 the bank says a break above the 21DMA at 1.3030 would likely see only temporary gains and it would look to sell such a move, looking for a return to the recent lows of 1.2765 and potentially 1.2650. EUR/CHF now at 1.3011. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP steady late; broad USD strength vs JPY, EUR offering support but continued equity fund inflows, growing overseas workers remittance lift local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair at 44.26, trading in narrow 44.11-44.43 band in heavy volume; traders expect USD/PHP to stay in 44.00-44.50 range in near term. "News that tax collection was off target in August may undermine the bullish sentiment for the peso tomorrow," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0837 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts little moved despite data showing jobless claims rose 2,300 in August vs expectations for a fall by 3,000, continuing recent run of slightly weak data in the UK. December gilts are down 0.20 at 123.03. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0507ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
