\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/CHF To Move In Range Near 1.30 Before SNB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 09:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0727 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF will trade a tight range around the 1.30 level ahead of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's interest-rate decision Thursday, says BayernLB. "We don't expect any SNB tightening tomorrow, as this will simply make the Swiss franc even more attractive, which the SNB won't want," says trader Roland Raczek. EUR/CHF has support on the downside at 1.2975, and upside resistance at around 1.3050, he adds. EUR/CHF trades at 1.3028. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] The dampening effect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had on the yen is set to peter out soon, a strategist says. "I don't think the yen will feel the impact long, much as the franc continues to surge despite previous SNB \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0726 GMT [Dow Jones] iTraxx Japan Series 13 falls to mid-indication of 96.5 bps vs yesterday's 99 bps as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened JPY, brightening outlook for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key export sector, says Japan bank trader; says, however, tightening move wasn't that large because "players had already had bullish views (over Japan firms), and there's caution ahead of the benchmark index's rollover" later this month. iTraxx actually traded at 99, 98 bps earlier in day. Single-name CDS deals done today include 4-year USD-denominated protection on Japanese sovereign debt at 50 bps vs 49 bps on Monday, \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2 . (6758.TO) at 49 bps vs 56 bps on Sept. 3, \uc2 Komatsu Ltd\uc2 . (6301.TO) at 43 bps. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0724 GMT [Dow Jones] September HSI futures down 0.1% at 21,671, mostly in line with cash market's 0.2% decline. Still, throughout session contract trading at small yet consistent premium to cash, now around 10 points, signaling view slight HSI pullback merely normal consolidation as HK market, having risen 9 out of past 10 sessions, may need breather. Volume light at 44,500 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] On first view, easy Japanese monetary policy and BOJ plans to leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unsterilized should help to keep the JPY down for now. But, it takes two to tango and with rising talk of Fed increasing QE, US policy will certainly not be pointed the right way. As \uc2 UBS\uc2  says, "although the overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should put a short term floor under USD/JPY, we expect the pair to remain heavy over the medium term as the market continues to worry about the possibility of further QE by the Fed." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0723 GMT [Dow Jones] "EUR/SEK implied volatility picked up Tuesday and will probably remain bid for the rest of the week due to the election and the fact that speculative accounts have been big buyers lately," says SEB. Adds stamina outside 9.1560/9.2400 is needed for a sense of direction. EUR/SEK trades at 9.2008. (charles.duxbury@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0722 GMT [Dow Jones] German bund 2.25% September 2020, on auction Wednesday for EUR5 billion, has "very little to offer" in terms of relative value as it is trading fairly expensive within the 10-year sector, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  analyst Elaine Lin, expecting an average take-up. Recent flow has not been in favor of bunds as peripheral spreads have been gapping in, but despite the recent tightening, they are still heavily exposed to event risks like issuance upsize or general market sentiment. "This uncertainty should keep demand (going)for flight-to-safety bids for German bunds," she says. (emese.bartha@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0719 GMT [Dow Jones] A very manic overnight session which began with the dollar almost collapsing against the majors in U.S. trade and culminated with USD/JPY spiking 200sen early Wednesday as the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 2004. The late selloff in the greenback Tuesday was supposedly triggered by a \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  conference call that the Fed could soon announce a new asset-purchase program. EUR/USD added 2 cents, GBP/USD one cent and USD/JPY slipped below 83.00. However the latter obviously didn't please the Japanese authorities who entered the market in Asia after a fresh 15 year low of 82.87 was seen and managed to push USD/JPY back above 85.00. The spot now trades at 85.11 and its unlikely that the market will try and test the authorities patience this early in the game so look for limited dips in USD/JPY and EUR/JPY ahead of the U.S. open. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] India's inflation likely to moderate further on drop in primary product prices due to good monsoon, favorable base effect, says Citi in report. India from Tuesday moved to FY05 as base year for calculating inflation from earlier FY94 and introduced new wholesale price index series including 676 items vs 435 earlier. August inflation print based on new index came in at 8.5% vs 9.8% in July. House revises expectations of average inflation in FY11 to 7.9% vs 8.8% earlier to adjust for new WPI series and new base year. It however, expects RBI to hike both repo, reverse repo rates by 25 bps on Thursday given macro trends in non-food credit, tax collections and sectoral data on autos, cement, diesel etc. House notes RBI saying that policy rates are still "far from a normal position." (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] Weakness in the euro zone peripheral bond sector is set to continue, driven by a combination of this week's disappointing euro zone data, ongoing political concerns in Belgium and further Irish underperformance, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets strategist Nishay Patel. "Peripheral vs core spreads remain vulnerable to further widening," he says. After Monday's successful Italian auctions, the bonds were weighed down Tuesday with the 10-year Italian/German yield spread rising 7 bps this week to 149 bps. The spread opens Wednesday at 149 bps, Spanish/German at 178 bps, Portuguese/German at 334 bps, Irish/German at 361 bps and Greek/German at 900 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] Likelihood that BOJ isn't moving to sterilize effect of MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on yen is important, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2 ; "the Fed has been aggressively expanding its balance sheet over the last two years. In a simple text book world this expands the supply of dollars, so the dollar falls. If the BOJ wants to arrest yen strength and address deflation, it needs to expand its balance sheet and print more yen. However, the BOJ has not been aggressive with its balance sheet. As of the end of August, the BOJ's balance sheet is only 10% larger than it was at the end of 2007." BOJ balance sheet was rising though before today's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  action and "it will rise even faster now. To be sure, how quickly it rises will be important for us. But at face value, we see finally reasons to buy" USD/JPY. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate briefly breaches RBI's key lending rate of 5.75%, but now lower as banks borrow from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily discount window to meet cash needs, says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 5.60% after climbing to 5.95% early session, 5.65% last close. Collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate at 5.76% vs 5.70% at open. Trader says, banking system liquidity has shifted to deficit mode on account of heavy withdrawals for corporate advance tax payments. Banks prefer to borrow from RBI vs call market as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  offers cash at fixed rate. Tips call rate in 5.60%-5.80% band in session. Indian banks borrowed INR161.55 billion from RBI Wednesday. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0327ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
