rformance, says \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets strategist Nishay Patel. "Peripheral vs core spreads remain vulnerable to further widening," he says. After Monday's successful Italian auctions, the bonds were weighed down Tuesday with the 10-year Italian/German yield spread rising 7 bps this week to 149 bps. The spread opens Wednesday at 149 bps, Spanish/German at 178 bps, Portuguese/German at 334 bps, Irish/German at 361 bps and Greek/German at 900 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0713 GMT [Dow Jones] Likelihood that BOJ isn't moving to sterilize effect of MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on yen is important, says \uc2 Westpac\uc2 ; "the Fed has been aggressively expanding its balance sheet over the last two years. In a simple text book world this expands the supply of dollars, so the dollar falls. If the BOJ wants to arrest yen strength and address deflation, it needs to expand its balance sheet and print more yen. However, the BOJ has not been aggressive with its balance sheet. As of the end of August, the BOJ's balance sheet is only 10% larger than it was at the end of 2007." BOJ balance sheet was rising though before today's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  action and "it will rise even faster now. To be sure, how quickly it rises will be important for us. But at face value, we see finally reasons to buy" USD/JPY. (rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] India interbank call rate briefly breaches RBI's key lending rate of 5.75%, but now lower as banks borrow from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's daily discount window to meet cash needs, says state-run bank trader. Overnight call rate at 5.60% after climbing to 5.95% early session, 5.65% last close. Collateralized borrowing and lending obligation rate at 5.76% vs 5.70% at open. Trader says, banking system liquidity has shifted to deficit mode on account of heavy withdrawals for corporate advance tax payments. Bank