\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/SGD Unlikely To Approach 1.3300 N/T -Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0711 GMT [Dow Jones] Line for USD/SGD has probably been drawn at 1.3300 with SGD having traded at top of likely MAS policy band for past few days, trader says. Doesn't expect pair to fall to that level any time in next few days as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in USD/JPY setting pretty clear downside boundaries for USD in Asian trade. "There's always this risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the MAS," trader says. Resistance likely at 1.3400, expects some cues may come from U.S. Treasury Secretary Geithner's comments on CNY later this week; pair last 1.3373. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] Calling time on the CHF rally, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. At the moment, there is still plenty of reason for bulls to continue buying the Swiss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Swiss economy continues to outperform its neighbors and uncertainty over the global recovery makes the franc attractive as a safe haven. But, this could well prove to be the last of the CHF's rally. Not only is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's safe-haven role likely to become less urgent, once the global recovery regains its traction, but the Swiss authorities are likely to become less tolerant as the Swiss economy itself slows down. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF instructs the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its behalf Wednesday, for the first time since March 2004, dragging USD/JPY from below Y83 to an intra-day high of Y85.14. "It is impossible to say how serious the MOF are or at what point they will ease off," says Guillaume Salomon at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 , "but we can at least say that the game has changed and the JPY will now take some time to settle." USD/JPY now at Y84.99. (peter.nurse@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0700 GMT [Dow Jones] The Spanish finance agency is scheduled to sell EUR3 billion 10-year bonds and EUR1 billion 30-year bonds Thursday taking the completed issuance for 2010 to around 72% of the required EUR96 billion, says Nishay Patel, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  Global Markets strategist. The 10-year bond cheapened versus German bunds Tuesday, with the yield spread widening to 177 bps. This reflects concession building ahead of the auction and Patel fails to see the attraction of the Spanish bonds. "The risk-reward of buying Spain outright or versus Germany looks unattractive to us." (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift its two key policy rates by 25 basis points each at Thursday's monetary policy review in bid to rein in inflationary expectations as economy continues to show robust growth. 10 of 18 economists polled by Dow Jones expect RBI to raise repo, reverse repo rate by 25 bps, while four expect 25 bps hike in only reverse repo. "The economy is on a roll, clearly, but so are price pressures. This requires a determined tap on the brake by policy-makers. Rates need to go higher still," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Frederic Neumann says. India's August inflation 9.5% on-year, slower than July 9.97%, below 9.6% estimated in Dow Jones poll, as per old series. Factory output for July +13.8%, well-above estimated 7.8%. Decision due 0630 GMT Thursday. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com, khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks generally still firm, with small caps continuing to lead gains. Market breadth at 2 gainers for every decliner. "Blue chips are being sidelined as traders zoom in on small caps and S-chips. There's a fair bit of momentum in the market," says dealer at foreign brokerage. FTSE ST Small Cap Index +0.9%, FTSE ST China Index +0.9% vs STI +0.4% at 3062.06. If year-to-date high of 3068 gives way, next resistance for STI expected at lower end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. Notable gainers among small caps, S-chips include \uc2 China Hongxing Sports\uc2  (BR9.SG), +7.5% at S$0.215, Yangzijiang (BS6.SG), +4.8% at S$1.75, Cosco (F83.SG), +3.1% at S$1.68, Informatics (I03.SG), +6.1% at S$0.175. Among blue chips, developers underperforming after fresh official data released during midday break showed developers sold fewer homes in August, 1,248 vs 1,549 in July. \uc2 CapitaLand\uc2  (C31.SG) off 0.5% at S$4.00, CityDev (C09.SG) flat at S$11.62. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0648 GMT [Dow Jones] China onshore IRS down, snapping 3-day winning streak, after U.S. treasury yields fall significantly. 1-year down 1 bp at 2.10%-2.17%, 5-year down 1 bp at 2.81%-2.88%. "Recent gains aren't sustainable as I see most traders are just taking profit on their long positions," says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. IRS up around 10 bps in past 3 days on robust August China economic data. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0647 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ policy board member Noda says BOJ will continue to provide ample funds into financial markets, also considering to making use of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds; comment suggests that BOJ may not soak up extra \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from market, leave \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unsterilized". If that's true, it'll strengthen BOJ's already-accommodative monetary policy even further and help show \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easing stance more clearly to market players. Noda adds, funds that entered the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market through today's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "have a temporary ability to provide money to the financial market." (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0646 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bonds' prices surge in moderate volume as foreign funds enter market after week-long holiday, dealers say. "In general, appetite for high-yielding assets in emerging markets is on the rise. The big jump for the bonds is simply because our market was closed during Eid holidays," dealer at local securities firm says; adds downward correction for short-dated bond likely in 4Q due to high inflation. Elsewhere foreigners buy IDR1.5 trillion ($168 million) worth of stocks. 5-year yield drops to 7.38% vs 7.59% on Sep. 7, before market's break; 10-year at 7.93% vs 8.13%; 15-year at 8.49% vs 8.72%; 20-year at 8.98% vs 9.20%. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI down 0.1% at 21,677.60, on mild profit-taking after index gained 9 out of past 10 sessions; volume solid at HK$40.22 billion. Still, pullback looks like normal consolidation; in view of current market sentiment, volume, HSI Index may next test early August high of 21,800, says South China Research, though adds that slew of fund-raising activities may "put off some investors." Blue chips largely in consolidation mode: only 3 stocks rising or falling more than 2%; \uc2 China Resources Enterprise\uc2  (0291.HK) down 3.1% at HK$32.50, succumbing to profit-taking after setting 52-week high yesterday, is worst-performing index member. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT [Dow Jones] The DMO auctions GBP2 Bln UKT 2030 Wednesday, ahead of a GBP3 Bln auction of 10-year gilts Thursday, and the timing could leave the sale susceptible, says Nomura. "The prevailing steepness in the curve, despite fundamentals, suggests that the gilt market may be struggling to fully digest the supply that has come before it despite what looks to be a lighter burden for the remainder of the fiscal year," Nomura says, noting the recent decline in bid-to-cover ratios at auctions. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are down Wednesday, with European equities set to open up tracking their Asian peers. Stocks in Asia were generally higher, led by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  where equities rose after authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to try to halt the JPY's rise. The market is also preparing for supply, with an EUR5B bund auction lined up later in the session. The data calendar sees euro-zone harmonized CPI and employment data at 0900 GMT, while in the US, the Empire State Index and industrial production figures are due at 1230 GMT and 1315 GMT, respectively. "With the manufacturing survey carrying more forward-looking information the macro support for bond markets should remain in place, especially as euro-zone core inflation is forecast to slip below 1% again," says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . December bunds are down 0.11 at 130.40 and the 10-year bund is down at 98.84 to yield 2.383%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0311ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
