\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Taiwan Shares End +0.4%; 8200 Resistance Thursday\b0\par\par\uc2 1,517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 08:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end +0.4% at 8163.82 with touch panel stocks leading gains; but profit-taking pressure mounting after benchmark index gained 4.2% in past 4 sessions, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. "Room for further gains is limited. 8200 will be the key resistance in the near term." Tips Taiex in tight 8110-8200 range Thursday. Adds, touch panel makers draw decent buying interest as touch screen gadgets have been well-received by market. Wintek (2384.NT) +7.0% at NT$49.15 but HTC (2498.TW) down 3.3% at NT$730.00 on profit-taking after having gained 17% in last 4 sessions. (lorraine.luk@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0606 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares off 1.3% at 909.76 at midday pause in active trade. Across-the-board fall weighed by worries whether \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  may impose measures to curb baht rise; sudden gains in USD/THB on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to dealers, in wake of Tokyo's JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also prompting investors to lock in profit, says Phillip Securities' assistant vice president Teerada Chanyingyong. Benchmark briefly hit 2-week-low of 902.46 intrasession before bargain hunting kicked, trimmed losses. "The index slipping through 915 support for today caused some short-term negative sentiment. If 900 psychological support cannot hold, this may lead to a big downward correction," Teerada says. Notes eyes on court's hearing today on legal challenge to 3G mobile license auction, with state-owned \uc2 CAT Telecom\uc2  seeking injunction. Resistance at 912. Among top actives, PTT (PTT.TH) down 1.4% at THB278, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 2.8% at THB104.50, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) down 3.4% at THB91.25, Banpu (BANPU.TH) down 0.6% at THB632. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0605 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's Bureau of Meteorology points out that current La Nina event in Pacific Basin, which usually associated with average to above average rainfall in eastern, northern Australia, coincides with negative Indian Ocean Dipole, which is usually associated with average to above average rainfall in southern Australia. Combination of both climatic events suggest winter crops in eastern Australia, including wheat and barley, could be in for a wet spring and possibly a wet harvest in November, December. If this comes to pass, quality of winter crops could be downgraded, such as through staining, or lower protein content for wheat, and so command a lower price on market. The Australian Oilseeds Federation reported today that in New South Wales and Victoria there is already sufficient soil moisture in most regions to carry the crop through to harvest, which suggest that these crops prone to damage if further heavy falls occur. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't the only place affected by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with Australian bonds falling on both ends of curve as well. "It did pressure our market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted Nikkei strength and broader Asian equity market rallies and that put some pressure on Aussie 3-years," says Sydney-based bond trader; reckons more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could mean more bond weakness. Aussie 3-year bonds recently off 3 ticks at 95.235. Also on session, Aussie bond contracts rolled over from September to December, though traders say MOF moves overshadowed roll over. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  is sticking to its forecast that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will ignore the potential bullish effect of an interest-rate increase on the Swiss franc, and lift its 3-month Swiss franc Libor target rate Thursday. The overwhelming consensus among most observers is that the SNB will keep rates at 0.25% when policy makers meet, with its year-end meeting more likely to result in a monetary tightening. "At this stage the CHF rate is the only monetary variable moving, thus pressuring the front end of the CHF yield curve even closer toward zero," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "While this isn't favourable for the SNB, the assumption of a material upward revised GDP forecast by the SNB means we see very strong arguments for a change in the SNB's target rate," he points out. EUR/CHF at 1.3005. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] Today's rallying AUD/JPY on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be end of gains for pair, says Kurt Magnus, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Nomura in Sydney. "With these type of actions, it give confidence in central banks trying to reflate global economies. This won't be a 24-hour phenomenon," says Magnus. AUD/JPY up to 79.595 recently from around 77.95 just prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, marking highest point since early August. AUD/USD should also be strong, says Magnus; adds, "there is every reason to continue buying Aussie, not just because of yield and carry advantage, but also because of the implications of an Asian-led growth story that has fed directly into commodities, especially gold and precious metals." AUD/USD recently at 0.9378 after reaching new two-year high of 0.9457 overnight. (geoff