\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: More Aussie Bond Weakness Could Come - Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 08:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0604 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weren't the only place affected by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , with Australian bonds falling on both ends of curve as well. "It did pressure our market. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prompted Nikkei strength and broader Asian equity market rallies and that put some pressure on Aussie 3-years," says Sydney-based bond trader; reckons more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could mean more bond weakness. Aussie 3-year bonds recently off 3 ticks at 95.235. Also on session, Aussie bond contracts rolled over from September to December, though traders say MOF moves overshadowed roll over. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0602 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UBS\uc2  is sticking to its forecast that the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  will ignore the potential bullish effect of an interest-rate increase on the Swiss franc, and lift its 3-month Swiss franc Libor target rate Thursday. The overwhelming consensus among most observers is that the SNB will keep rates at 0.25% when policy makers meet, with its year-end meeting more likely to result in a monetary tightening. "At this stage the CHF rate is the only monetary variable moving, thus pressuring the front end of the CHF yield curve even closer toward zero," says economist Reto Huenerwadel. "While this isn't favourable for the SNB, the assumption of a material upward revised GDP forecast by the SNB means we see very strong arguments for a change in the SNB's target rate," he points out. EUR/CHF at 1.3005. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] Today's rallying AUD/JPY on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be end of gains for pair, says Kurt Magnus, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Nomura in Sydney. "With these type of actions, it give confidence in central banks trying to reflate global economies. This won't be a 24-hour phenomenon," says Magnus. AUD/JPY up to 79.595 recently from around 77.95 just prior to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  news, marking highest point since early August. AUD/USD should also be strong, says Magnus; adds, "there is every reason to continue buying Aussie, not just because of yield and carry advantage, but also because of the implications of an Asian-led growth story that has fed directly into commodities, especially gold and precious metals." AUD/USD recently at 0.9378 after reaching new two-year high of 0.9457 overnight. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trims gains on export settlements, foreign equity fund inflows, traders say. Pair last at 1,162.3 vs 1,166.5 earlier high, but likely to remain above 1,161.7 late yesterday in Seoul due to expected corporate demand related to recent M&A deals. Players also remain wary of possible BOK \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (to support pair) following \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier to weaken JPY. Local bank trader expects USD/KRW to consolidate around current levels for rest of session. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0541 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian shares +3.8% at 3353.537 midday in heavy volume as foreign investors buy after week-long holiday, dealers say. "Sentiment is positive but gains will likely be limited in the afternoon session," dealer says. Foreigners net buyers in local bourse today, picking up about IDR1.5 trillion ($168 million) worth of shares. Among gainers, \uc2 Astra International\uc2  (ASII.JK) +8.9% at IDR58,250; Telkom (TLKM.JK) +4.4% at IDR9,400; \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) +9.5% at IDR6,900 after \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  raised its target price; \uc2 Indofood\uc2  (INDF.JK) +7.7% at IDR5,250; Bumi (BUMI.JK) +5.2% at IDR1,810. 3,300-3,380 band tipped for rest of session.(andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to take a rate action on Thursday even though August inflation, as per new wholesale price index series, shows larger-than-expected fall, says \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . "Inflationary expectations are likely to be still relatively elevated since in level terms food prices are yet to see a notable correction," says house. Adds, consumer faces higher fuel prices across the board, from price hikes implemented in July. House expects base case 25 bp hike in only reverse repo rate but does not rule out hike in repo rate. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's jobless rate likely held steady at 4.3% in June-August period, according to median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Still, economists say it could fall toward year-end as city's economy continues to grow. "Domestic demand remains robust due to the strength of the local economy, and the situation surrounding Europe's sovereign debt crisis is better-than-expected, which helped to support Hong Kong's labor market," says \uc2 Bank of East Asia\uc2  economist Paul Tang. Tips jobless rate to fall to 4.0% by year-end. Data due 0830 GMT Thursday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] After 6-year absence, Japan's MOF has returned to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell JPY; this time BOJ is cooperating by not sterilizing the surplus liquidity. However, Japan is taking on largest 24-hour market in world so success may be measured on more modest scale than in previous battles. Wherever USD/JPY is heading from here, next 24 hours may be crucial in changing psychology of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. See: =MONEY TALKS: Japan Takes On The $4 Trillion Monster (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0527 GMT [Dow Jones] Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE) President Atsushi Saito welcomes \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "What is the most important thing for Japan now is to have a decisive stance and show \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators that measures can be taken to counter the unnatural state of the yen's rise." Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely "symptomatic therapy" (measure to treat symptoms, not cause) for deflation, hopes Kan administration to come up with solid growth strategy to counter Japan's ongoing deflationary problem. Nikkei up 2.8%, USD/JPY at 84.99. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] After 6-year absence Japan's MOF has returned to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell JPY; this time BOJ is cooperating by not sterilizing the surplus liquidity. However, Japan is taking on largest 24-hour market in world so success may be measured on more modest scale than in previous battles. Wherever USD/JPY is heading from here, next 24 hours may be crucial in changing psychology of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds up, tracking rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, while investors turn cautious as they try to figure out how \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will affect TWD, and overall domestic economy, says trader from local brokerage. Adds, bonds unlikely to rise sharply with no strong lead from local stock market to help affirm clear direction. Still, "the economic outlook is not great so the new 10-year bond yield should remain low." Tips 1.20%-1.25% range at 10-year bond auction tomorrow. 10-year yield last at 1.2260% vs 1.2401% yesterday, tipped in 1.21%-1.23% Thursday. 5-year yield last at 0.8850% vs 0.8860%. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD steady after drifting off all day from overnight high of 0.7396; "It seems to be stabilizing at the moment, and I think it is probably still a buy on dips, but you may get support around 0.7270 and that may be a bit of an opportunity," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  NZ Chief Manager Daniel Brdanovic. Pair last at 0.7321. Says NZD, AUS, EUR likely to find further support after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY earlier and "it seems to be a risk on environment at the moment." Tips initial resistance at overnight high. Says market expecting RBNZ to remain on hold at tomorrow's rate review and while may be dovish on economy given weak data of late, earthquake, failure of South Canterbury Finance, may be more upbeat about what's happening offshore. "Net-net, it may be a fairly balanced outlook." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0204ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f00040\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
