\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:HK Jun-Aug Jobless Rate Likely Steady At 4.3%-Poll\b0\par\par\uc2 1,397 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2010\par 07:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0540 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Hong Kong's jobless rate likely held steady at 4.3% in June-August period, according to median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones. Still, economists say it could fall toward year-end as city's economy continues to grow. "Domestic demand remains robust due to the strength of the local economy, and the situation surrounding Europe's sovereign debt crisis is better-than-expected, which helped to support Hong Kong's labor market," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of East Asia\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Paul Tang. Tips jobless rate to fall to 4.0% by year-end. Data due 0830 GMT Thursday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] After 6-year absence, Japan's MOF has returned to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell JPY; this time BOJ is cooperating by not sterilizing the surplus liquidity. However, Japan is taking on largest 24-hour market in world so success may be measured on more modest scale than in previous battles. Wherever USD/JPY is heading from here, next 24 hours may be crucial in changing psychology of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. See: =MONEY TALKS: Japan Takes On The $4 Trillion Monster (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0527 GMT [Dow Jones] Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE) President Atsushi Saito welcomes \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "What is the most important thing for Japan now is to have a decisive stance and show \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators that measures can be taken to counter the unnatural state of the yen's rise." Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is merely "symptomatic therapy" (measure to treat symptoms, not cause) for deflation, hopes Kan administration to come up with solid growth strategy to counter Japan's ongoing deflationary problem. Nikkei up 2.8%, USD/JPY at 84.99. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0520 GMT [Dow Jones] After 6-year absence Japan's MOF has returned to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell JPY; this time BOJ is cooperating by not sterilizing the surplus liquidity. However, Japan is taking on largest 24-hour market in world so success may be measured on more modest scale than in previous battles. Wherever USD/JPY is heading from here, next 24 hours may be crucial in changing psychology of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0519 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds up, tracking rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, while investors turn cautious as they try to figure out how \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will affect TWD, and overall domestic economy, says trader from local brokerage. Adds, bonds unlikely to rise sharply with no strong lead from local stock market to help affirm clear direction. Still, "the economic outlook is not great so the new 10-year bond yield should remain low." Tips 1.20%-1.25% range at 10-year bond auction tomorrow. 10-year yield last at 1.2260% vs 1.2401% yesterday, tipped in 1.21%-1.23% Thursday. 5-year yield last at 0.8850% vs 0.8860%. (paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0518 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD steady after drifting off all day from overnight high of 0.7396; "It seems to be stabilizing at the moment, and I think it is probably still a buy on dips, but you may get support around 0.7270 and that may be a bit of an opportunity," says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  NZ Chief Manager Daniel Brdanovic. Pair last at 0.7321. Says NZD, AUS, EUR likely to find further support after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on USD/JPY earlier and "it seems to be a risk on environment at the moment." Tips initial resistance at overnight high. Says market expecting RBNZ to remain on hold at tomorrow's rate review and while may be dovish on economy given weak data of late, earthquake, failure of South Canterbury Finance, may be more upbeat about what's happening offshore. "Net-net, it may be a fairly balanced outlook." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0459 GMT [Dow Jones] Macquarie says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is encouraging as it shows increased attention to risks to economic cycle; says implication is that other complementary policies might also be seen. But adds, European and U.S. officials unlikely to be thrilled by Japan's action. Says there is a risk that if U.S. embarks on another quantitative easing that "we end up in a developed world beggar-thy-neighbour liquidity war. This could be very inflationary for developing world risk assets and commodity prices." (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely to hike rates three times this year including 25 basis points at mid-quarter review Thursday, says Daiwa Capital Markets in report. "We view inflationary expectations as well-entrenched now, and needing to be wrestled lower," says house; adds, real rates in economy deeply negative. House expects further 25 basis points hike in repo rate in next two scheduled policy reviews. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares end higher, supported by gains in mining, utility stocks; benchmark PSEi ends +0.1% at 3973.48 in heavy volume. Market breadth positive with gainers beating losers 74 to 64. Among leading gainers, telecom firm PLDT (TEL.PH) +1.4% at PHP2,498, geothermal power producer Energy Development (EDC.PH) +2.1% at PHP5.78, power firm First Gen (FGEN.PH) +4.3% at PHP13.62. Profit-taking hits Banco de Oro (BDO.PH), down 0.7% to PHP60.50, Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH), down 1.1% to PHP73. Joseph Roxas, president of Eagle Equities, says foreign funds continue to flow into market with mining plays receiving rotational interest. Tips 3900-4000 range in near term. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0455 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan MOF bought USD/JPY above 85.00, say 3 dealers at major financial firms; pair has gone as high as 85.14, and last at 85.10. One says sharp move above 85.00 would be difficult, though, due to "massive...I mean massive" offers from Japanese exporters; "naturally when we have this much selling offers the dollar can't rise (much) above 85.00." Notes further resistance at 86.00. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0451 GMT [Dow Jones] As India's quest for coal heats up, it may need to lock horns more tightly with bigger neighbor China to secure enough supply of fuel to feed its fast-expanding economy. India, China consumed over 60% of global coal output last year, vast additions of new thermal-power capacity, steel-making plants likely to continue driving demand over at least next 2 decades. Much depends on both countries' strategies to secure overseas coal supplies or make most of their own deposits. However, India's strategy of relying ever more heavily on traditional suppliers may be unsustainable. Indonesia's government wants to conserve more of its coal output for domestic use, and labor, transport issues persist in South Africa. "Much like in Indonesia, where the government has issued a domestic market obligation to protect the country's domestic interests thereby placing restrictions on coal exports, countries like South Africa and Russia may suffer from the same predicament," says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . See: =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: India, China Vie For Coal Market Supremacy (eric.yep@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI down 0.3% at 1470.72 midday on profit-taking after recent highs, ahead of national holiday Thursday, dealer says. Tuesday, KLCI ended at fresh 32-month high of 1,474.44. "There is some profit-taking in blue-chips after the rally over the last few weeks," says dealer; tips for 1,465-1,474 range for rest of session. Among decliners Jerneh (6394.KU) down 5.5% at MYR3.10 on concerns over company's future following sale of core insurance business; AMMB (1015.KU) down 1.3% at MYR5.90, Genting (3182.KU) down 0.5% at MYR9.94. Among gainers Gamuda (5398.KU) +0.8% at MYR3.73 after news of being picked to be KLCI component stock from Sept. 20, TA Ann Holdings (5012.KU) +1.5% at MYR4.82. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com) [ 09-15-10 0140ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100915e69f0003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
